16:13:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really fair follow-up — and you’re right, it’s not just big cities. Many smaller towns and even suburban neighborhoods have 3D models accurate down to the windows. It does seem bananas that someone would send a plane over a little town just so you can count your window panes in 3D. But there’s a practical, less-romantic reason it happens. ### The economics of aerial surveys: they don’t fly *just* for windows Aerial imagery companies don’t plan a flight path saying, “We need the window count on 123 Maple Street.” They win contracts to map **entire regions**, often for government, insurance, real estate, and environmental monitoring. A typical project might be: - **State-wide or county-wide orthoimagery:** Many U.S. states commission a full aerial survey every 2–5 years for tax assessment, emergency planning, and infrastructure. The plane covers the entire area at a uniform resolution — every farm, small town, suburb, and city. If Peachville (pop. 4,000) happens to lie in the flight grid, it gets captured with the same 7–15 cm resolution as the big city. - **National mapping programs:** In Europe, for example, countries like the Netherlands and Germany have regular aerial surveys of the whole territory. - **Google’s own projects (or licensing deals):** Google has directly flown or licensed large swaths of the U.S., Europe, and other regions. They don’t pick and choose individual streets; they fly huge blocks, often hundreds of square miles at a time. So when you see a small town in full 3D glory, it’s because it happened to be inside one of these massive regional sweeps. The planes didn’t go out of their way; they just included it in the grid. ### Why go through all that processing for a small town? The photogrammetry pipeline is almost entirely automated. Once you have the high-resolution aerial imagery with proper overlap, software can churn through the whole area and spit out 3D meshes for everything — skyscrapers, churches, gas stations, houses. The marginal cost of including a small town is negligible; the computers don’t care whether it’s Manhattan or a village of 800 people. And the result is a more complete product. For Google Earth, the more 3D coverage the better, because it makes the experience “seamless.” You can fly from a city center to a rural village and keep the 3D layer on without an abrupt change in quality (at least in places they’ve flown). ### Small print: not all small towns have 3D, and sometimes it’s coarser If you wander around Google Earth, you’ll also find plenty of small towns with no 3D buildings at all — just flat 2D imagery with the “Satellite” label. That’s because they weren’t included in any recent aerial survey with the right angle configurations, or the available data lacks the multi-view overlap needed for photogrammetry. In those cases, Google might use lower-resolution satellite-derived terrain (which shows landscape shapes but not individual building windows). So if you *do* see windows in a small town, it’s a pretty clear sign a plane flew over. ### In short You’re not crazy: small towns absolutely get the full-blown 3D treatment, windows and all. It’s just a happy side effect of large-scale aerial mapping programs, not a targeted mission to count your bathroom window. The planes capture everything in their path, and then the automated algorithms reconstruct the entire area without any manual “add windows here” step. It feels bespoke, but it’s mass-produced — which is its own kind of impressive.
